 and splitting - rules\ndocs/editors-guide/ClinGen-lumping-splitting.md:8:The Clinical Genome Resource (ClinGen) has developed frameworks to determine whether diseases should be “lumped” or “split”. [2].\ndocs/editors-guide/ClinGen-lumping-splitting.md:10:In the context of the collaboration between ClinGen and Mondo, many requests refer to “lumping” or “splitting” terms in Mondo. This document summarized the rules followed in Mondo.\ndocs/editors-guide/ClinGen-lumping-splitting.md:23:However, the [ClinGen Retina Gene Curation Expert Panel](https://clinicalgenome.org/affiliation/40072/) consider these diseases as a single disease entity, and suggested these diseases to be lumped under the name \"RPE65-related recessive retinopathy\" (https://github.com/monarch-initiative/mondo/issues/4937)\ndocs/editors-guide/ClinGen-lumping-splitting.md:38:The [ClinGen Hemoglobinopathy Variant Curation Expert Panel](https://www.clinicalgenome.org/affiliation/50052/) suggested that different disease entitites were represented within this single OMIM disease (https://github.com/monarch-initiative/mondo/issues/7561)\nsrc/patterns/data/matches/infantile.tsv:4:http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0017354\tinfantile glycine encephalopathy\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0011612\tglycine encephalopathy\r\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/README.md:653:*This pattern is for diseases that are caused by a single variation or mutation in a single gene, that have gene-based names, such as new disease terms that are requested by ClinGen, like MED12-related intellectual disability syndrome. See more details about [disease naming in Mondo here](https://mondo.monarchinitiative.org/pages/disease-naming/).  Examples: [MED12-related intellectual disability syndrome](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0100000), [TTN-related myopathy](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0100175), [MYPN-related myopathy](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0015023)*\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/README.md:867:Examples: [infant botulism](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0015804), [infantile glycine encephalopathy](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0017354)*\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/README.md:883:| MONDO:0017354 | infantile glycine encephalopathy         | MONDO:0011612 | glycine encephalopathy         |\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/disease_series_by_gene.yaml:7:  by ClinGen, like MED12-related intellectual disability syndrome. See more details about [disease naming in Mondo here](https://mondo.monarchinitiative.org/pages/disease-naming/).  Examples: [MED12-related\ndocs/editors-guide/f-entities.md:45:  - if the original term label comes from OMIM, the original label is retained and the requested new label is added as an \"exact synonym\". If the request comes for a ClinGen working group, the synonym type called ‘ClinGen label’ will be added.  \ndocs/editors-guide/f-entities.md:85:The definition of the synonym is the same as the primary definition, but has additional qualifiers. **Note** - ClinGen curators will sometimes request narrow synoyms that are actually subclasses of the term. We can add narrow synonyms to help the users find the right term when it makes sense. For instance, when the synonym refers to another disease that is more specific, but in practice, people would use the more general term.\ndocs/editors-guide/f-entities.md:86:This is exactly the use case for ClinGen: since they lump 2 diseases, they would suspect that the narrow synonyms refer to the lumped term. See example: MONDO:0100582 'TOR1AIP1-related myopathy' (ticket: https://github.com/monarch-initiative/mondo/issues/8593).\ndocs/editors-guide/patterns/disease_series_by_gene.md:6:This pattern is for diseases that are caused by a single variation or mutation in a single gene, that have gene-based names, such as new disease terms that are requested by ClinGen, like MED12-related intellectual disability syndrome. See more details about [disease naming in Mondo here](https://mondo.monarchinitiative.org/pages/disease-naming/).  Examples: [MED12-related intellectual disability syndrome](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0100000), [TTN-related myopathy](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0100175), [MYPN-related myopathy](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0015023)\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/infantile.yaml:9:  glycine encephalopathy](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0017354)'\ndocs/editors-guide/patterns/infantile.md:8:Examples: [infant botulism](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0015804), [infantile glycine encephalopathy](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0017354)\ndocs/editors-guide/patterns/infantile.md:35:| [MONDO:0017354](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0017354) | infantile glycine encephalopathy         | [MONDO:0011612](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0011612) | glycine encephalopathy         |\ndocs/editors-guide/patterns/index.md:37:| [disease_series_by_gene](disease_series_by_gene/) | This pattern is for diseases that are caused by a single variation or mutation in a single gene, that have gene-based names, such as new disease terms that are requested by ClinGen, like MED12-related intellectual disability syndrome. See more details about [disease naming in Mondo here](https://mondo.monarchinitiative.org/pages/disease-naming/).  Examples: [MED12-related intellectual disability syndrome](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0100000), [TTN-related myopathy](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0100175), [MYPN-related myopathy](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0015023) | \ndocs/editors-guide/patterns/index.md:46:| [infantile](infantile/) | An instance of a disease that has an onset of signs or symptoms of disease within the first 12 months of life (infantile onset).<br/>Examples: [infant botulism](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0015804), [infantile glycine encephalopathy](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0017354) | \ndocs/editors-guide/merging-and-obsoleting.md:66:1. Run the [risky_obsoletion_check](https://mondo.readthedocs.io/en/latest/editors-guide/risky_obsoletion_check) pipeline to check if external ontologies and databases (such as EFO and ClinGen) are using this Mondo term.\ndocs/editors-guide/merging-and-obsoleting.md:67:    1. if any terms are reported, create a ticket on the [EFO tracker](https://github.com/EBISPOT/efo/issues) and notify ClinGen users (_for now, email Larry Babb_).\ndocs/editors-guide/synching.md:16:1. We may get disease to gene associations from external groups as well, such as ClinGen, who often request new gene-related disease terms.\ndocs/editors-guide/risky_obsoletion_check.md:5:The pipeline outlined below will check if external databases or ontologies (such as ClinGen or EFO) are using our Mondo terms in their projects. If so, we should reconsider if we want to obsolete these terms. If we do, we need to notify them that the terms are candidates for obsoletion.\ndocs/editors-guide/risky_obsoletion_check.md:14:  1. Share with ClinGen via email\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:81:# description: This QC check ensures that no class ever has two clingen labels, and that there are only exact preferred labels\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:413:      \"ClinGen\",\ndocs/editors-guide/sop_splitting-terms.md:8:1. Check to see if the term is being used by EFO, ClinGen or other users, where \n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
